  "pattern": "mutable-state",
  "difficulty_rating": 1625,
  "explanation": "`const active = items` binds a second name to the exact same array — it does not copy it. The reverse-iteration splice pattern used inside the loop is itself the textbook-correct way to remove-while-iterating, which makes the real bug easy to miss: the function mutates the caller's `original` array in place via that alias, so `filtered` and `original` end up identical (`original === filtered` is true) and `original.length` has shrunk to 1, something the caller almost certainly did not expect from a function that looks like it 'filters'.",
  "prompt": "removeExpired is meant to return a filtered copy without touching the caller's array. Step through and predict original.length, filtered.length, and whether they're the same object.",
  "language": "javascript",
  "snippet": "function removeExpired(items, now) {\n  const active = items;\n  for (let i = active.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n    if (active[i].expires < now) {\n      active.splice(i, 1);\n    }\n  }\n  return active;\n}\nconst original = [{ name: 'a', expires: 5 }, { name: 'b', expires: 20 }];\nconst filtered = removeExpired(original, 10);\nconsole.log(original.length, filtered.length, original === filtered);",
  "interaction": "scrubber",
